WORLD BANK INDEPENDENT EVALUATION GROUP (IEG)
Adaptive Project Design in Fragile and Conflict-Affected Situations: What Works
IEG Learning Product | 2024 | Report No: IEG-2024-FCS-001
Source: ieg.worldbankgroup.org | Portfolio Review: 52 FCS Operations (2015-2023)

EXECUTIVE SUMMARY:
This learning product synthesizes evidence from 52 World Bank operations in Fragile and
Conflict-Affected Situations (FCS) completed between 2015-2023. The core finding: projects
designed with explicit adaptive mechanisms achieved 68% of their PDO targets on average,
compared to 41% for traditionally designed projects in the same country contexts.

DEFINING ADAPTIVE DESIGN IN PRACTICE:
Adaptive design is not the absence of design — it is explicit planning for uncertainty. The
most effective adaptive mechanisms observed across the portfolio were:

1. CONTINGENCY COMPONENTS: Unallocated budget (10-20% of project cost) that can be deployed
   for emerging priorities without requiring formal restructuring
2. RESTRUCTURING TRIGGERS: Pre-agreed criteria that automatically initiate portfolio review
   (e.g., security incidents affecting >30% of project areas, political transitions)
3. THEORY OF CHANGE FLEXIBILITY: PDOs framed around outcomes rather than outputs,
   allowing implementation modality changes without PDO revision
4. THIRD-PARTY MONITORING: Independent verification that does not rely on government systems
   that may be compromised in conflict settings

KEY FINDING 1: SPEED OF INITIAL DISBURSEMENT DETERMINES COMMUNITY TRUST
In post-conflict and transition contexts, the speed of first disbursement is the single strongest
predictor of community participation in subsequent project phases. Projects achieving first
disbursement within 90 days of effectiveness showed 39% higher community participation rates
in Years 2-5 compared to projects with first disbursement after 180 days.

The mechanism: in communities with high institutional distrust, rapid visible delivery of resources
signals credibility. Delays are interpreted as evidence that "this program is like all the others"
— leading to disengagement that is very difficult to reverse.

Recommendation: FCS projects should be designed to enable quick-disbursing activities in the
first 90 days, even if larger infrastructure components require longer preparation.

KEY FINDING 2: REMOTE MONITORING TRIANGULATION IS THE RELIABILITY STANDARD
Single-method monitoring failed in 21 of 52 FCS operations reviewed. The most reliable monitoring
approach observed combined three independent sources:
(1) Satellite imagery for infrastructure verification
(2) Community scorecards for service quality perception
(3) Third-party field monitors for physical verification

Projects using all three methods showed 94% concordance with actual outcomes when independently
audited. Single-method projects showed only 61% concordance.

KEY FINDING 3: NGO IMPLEMENTATION PARTNERSHIPS REQUIRE EXPLICIT CAPACITY TRANSFER PLANS
Heavy reliance on international NGOs for implementation was observed in 38 of 52 operations.
In 14 of these, government capacity was assessed as lower at project close than at appraisal —
the NGO had effectively substituted for, rather than built, government capacity.

The distinction between high-performing and low-performing NGO partnerships was the presence
of explicit capacity transfer plans with measurable milestones. Projects with documented capacity
transfer plans (staff training completion, system handover dates, government counterpart ratios)
showed significantly better government ownership at close.

KEY FINDING 4: POLITICAL ECONOMY ANALYSIS MUST BE UPDATED ANNUALLY — NOT JUST AT APPRAISAL
Political economy analyses conducted at appraisal were found to be largely obsolete within
18-24 months in FCS contexts due to the rate of political change. Projects that commissioned
annual political economy updates were better able to anticipate stakeholder shifts, adjust
implementation modalities, and avoid being caught in political crossfire.

Cost of annual political economy update: approximately $15,000-25,000.
Cost of a project derailed by unanticipated political change: measured in years of delay and
tens of millions in preparation costs and disbursed funds at risk.

KEY FINDING 5: PROCUREMENT THRESHOLDS SHOULD BE RAISED FOR FCS CONTEXTS
Standard World Bank procurement thresholds are calibrated for functioning market environments.
In FCS contexts, the number of qualified bidders is systematically lower, and the transaction
costs of competitive procurement are higher relative to contract values.

The portfolio review found that raising National Shopping thresholds to $500,000 (from the
standard $100,000-200,000) in FCS contexts reduced average procurement time by 4.2 months
per contract cycle without increasing fraud or quality risks (based on audits of 340 contracts
across 18 FCS projects).

KEY FINDING 6: DO-NO-HARM ANALYSIS REQUIRES ONGOING UPDATE
Do-No-Harm analyses conducted at appraisal were rarely updated during implementation in the
projects reviewed. In 8 cases, project activities had unintended conflict-exacerbating effects
that were identified only through third-party monitoring — not through the Bank's supervision.

Recommendation: Do-No-Harm analysis should be a standing agenda item at every ISR preparation,
with a standardized template that takes no more than 2 hours to complete for TTLs familiar
with the context.

CONCLUSION:
The evidence strongly supports investment in adaptive design mechanisms. The marginal cost of
building in flexibility (contingency components, restructuring triggers, annual PEA updates)
is consistently lower than the cost of restructuring operations that have gone off track.
The FCS portfolio as a whole would benefit from standardizing adaptive design elements as
default features rather than exceptions to be justified case by case.
